  "response": "Adjudicating between the stimulus account and the supply-disruption account of the recent inflation surge is at bottom a problem of attribution: both candidate causes were present at unusual magnitudes, so the dispute turns on timing, on sectoral composition, and on the size of the measured impulses rather than on any single headline number. The M2 money stock in the United States grew by roughly 25 percent during 2020, and the New York Fed's Global Supply Chain Pressure Index reached its all-time high in December 2021; a monetary expansion of that scale cannot be squared with the supply-pressure gauge topping out at that date. The same index, by contrast, had returned to its historical average level during the first half of 2021.\n\nThe premises on which the two accounts rest are not mutually exclusive: at least one of the two must hold, and perhaps both \u2014 pandemic shutdowns reduced the productive capacity of global goods supply chains, and aggregate household demand in the United States ran above its pre-pandemic trend level during 2021. That curtailment of capacity in goods supply chains is what drove the pressure gauge to its December high. Economists at the San Francisco Fed estimated that US fiscal support added about three percentage points to core inflation by the end of 2021. Core goods inflation in the United States accelerated before services inflation did. The excess of household spending over its pre-pandemic path is precisely what indicates a fiscal contribution of that magnitude. That core goods prices turned up ahead of services prices, in turn, shows that about half of the above-target inflation in 2021 and 2022 was attributable to supply-side factors; in other words, supply constraints accounted for roughly 50 percent of the excess inflation recorded in 2021 and 2022.\n\nThe international record sharpens the comparison, because the same global shocks struck economies with very different fiscal and monetary stances. Euro area inflation peaked above 15 percent in 2022. Because Russia's invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 sharply raised global energy and food commodity prices, the result was a peak in US consumer price index inflation of 9.1 percent year-over-year in June 2022. That same commodity shock is what carried euro area prices to their peak, and the European high stands in sharp contrast to the American one.\n\nThe ratio of job vacancies to unemployed workers in the United States reached a record of about two in March 2022. Measures of long-run inflation expectations in the United States remained close to 2 percent throughout the inflation surge. The decisive evidence, then, is not any one series but the joint configuration of them: the dates at which supply pressure and price pressure peaked, the sectoral order in which prices moved, the estimated size of the fiscal impulse, and the behavior of expectations and of the labor market as the surge unfolded. Read together, these magnitudes assign a substantial share of the episode to constrained supply and a comparable share to the demand generated by monetary and fiscal support, which is why the working macroeconomist treats the two accounts as partial rather than strictly rival descriptions of the same episode.",
